# AWS {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}EC2{{ else }}serverless{{ end }} digest

Distilled from {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}the EC2 Auto Scaling user guide, the Application Load
Balancer guide, the Amazon VPC guide (endpoints, flow logs){{ else }}the Lambda developer guide's best practices and Node.js
handler pages, API Gateway's HTTP API docs{{ end }}, DynamoDB's developer guide, the
AWS SDK for JavaScript v3 guide, the CDK developer guide and its assertions
module, and CDK Nag's rule list (see `SOURCES.md`). `[lint: x]` = enforced by
`make check`: an ESLint rule or the compiler; `[nag: x]` = a CDK Nag rule that
fails `cdk synth`; `[test]` = an assertion in `infra/standard.test.ts` or a
stack's own test; `[review]` = check it yourself. `docs/well-architected.md`
says why each rule matters.

{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}## The server

- `src/main.ts` is the entry point the instances run (`node server.mjs`
  under systemd): it reads the environment, creates the SDK clients and the
  store, and starts the server from `src/server.ts`. [review]
- Create SDK clients and read configuration once, at start-up; every
  request reuses them and their connections. Never create a client per
  request. [review]
- Read `process.env` only in `src/main.ts`, once, and fail at start-up with a
  message naming the variable when one is missing: systemd restarts the
  server, and the load balancer's health check keeps traffic off it. [review]
- `src/server.ts` adapts HTTP to `answer` and nothing else: it reads the body
  up to a limit (413 past it), takes the request id from the load balancer's
  `X-Amzn-Trace-Id`, and writes one log entry per request. The work is in
  functions that take what they need (a store, a log), so tests call them
  without a server. [review]
- `GET /health` answers 200 without touching DynamoDB: it says the process
  serves, and a DynamoDB outage must not take every instance out of the
  target group at once. [review]
- The server's keep-alive outlasts the load balancer's 60-second idle
  timeout (`keepAliveTimeout` 65 s, `headersTimeout` above it), or a request
  racing a close gets a 502. [test]
- On `SIGTERM` (an instance being replaced or scaled in) stop accepting
  connections, finish the requests in flight, then let the process end.
  Never `process.exit()`. [lint: no-restricted-properties]
- Await every promise, or void it on purpose with a comment: a rejected
  promise nobody awaits ends the process.
  [lint: @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises]
- Don't keep request data in module-scope variables: the next request on the
  same instance would see it, and other instances would not. [review]

{{ else }}## The handler

- `src/handler.ts` exports `handler`, which takes an `APIGatewayProxyEventV2`
  and returns a result: a status code, headers and a string body. API
  Gateway's HTTP API sends payload format 2.0. [review]
- Create SDK clients and read configuration at module scope, once per
  execution environment; warm invocations reuse them and their connections.
  Never create a client inside the handler. [review]
- Read `process.env` only in `src/handler.ts`, once, and fail at start-up
  with a message naming the variable when one is missing. [review]
- Keep the handler to wiring; the work is in functions that take what they
  need (a store, a log), so tests call them without Lambda. [review]
- Await every promise before returning: work left running after the handler
  returns is frozen with the environment and may never finish.
  [lint: @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises]
- Never `process.exit()`: it kills the execution environment mid-invocation.
  [lint: no-restricted-properties]
- Don't keep request data in module-scope variables: the next invocation in
  the same environment would see it. [review]

{{ end }}## Requests and responses

- Validate every input at the boundary: path parameters, query strings,
  headers and the body, narrowed from `unknown` with runtime checks. {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}The load balancer passes on{{ else }}API
  Gateway delivers{{ end }} whatever the client sent. [review]
{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}{{ else }}- A body can arrive base64-encoded (`isBase64Encoded`); decode it before
  parsing. [review]
{{ end }}- Answer expected failures with their status: 400 for bad input, 403, 404,
  409 for a conflict, 413 for too large, and 500 only for a bug or an
  outage. The body is JSON with a `message` a client can act on. [review]
- Never return a stack trace, an SDK error's text or an internal name to a
  client; log it instead, with the request id. [review]
- Set `content-type` on every response. [review]

## Logs

- Log structured JSON, one entry per line, through the `Log` the code is
  given; never `console`. [lint: no-console]
- Each entry has a `level`, a `message` and the request id; errors say what
  failed and with which input, never a secret, a token or user data. [review]
{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}- The server writes to standard output; systemd appends it to
  `/var/log/server/server.log`, and the CloudWatch agent ships that file to
  the log group the stack creates, with a retention, one stream per
  instance. [test: log groups keep logs for a set time]
- The load balancer writes access logs to an S3 bucket, and the VPC's
  rejected traffic goes to a flow log. [nag: AwsSolutions-ELB2,
  AwsSolutions-VPC7]
{{ else }}- The function's log group is created by the stack, with a retention.
  [nag: none; test: log groups keep logs for a set time]
- The function logs in JSON format (`loggingFormat: LoggingFormat.JSON`), so
  Lambda's own entries are structured too. [review]
{{ end }}
## DynamoDB

- Model the table for its access patterns: every read on a request path is
  a `GetItem` or a `Query` on a key or an index, never a `Scan`. [review]
- Use the document client (`@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb`) so items are plain
  objects; validate what a read returns before using it, since another
  writer may have stored something else. [review]
- Writes are idempotent: a `PutItem` of the whole item, or a conditional
  write (`ConditionExpression`) that refuses a second apply. Never a blind
  `ADD` on a retried request. [review]
- Tables are on demand (`PAY_PER_REQUEST`) unless the issue states a steady
  load. [review]
- Point-in-time recovery on. [nag: AwsSolutions-DDB3]
- Encrypted at rest with a KMS key (`TableEncryption.AWS_MANAGED` or
  customer-managed). [test: encrypts every table]
- The table outlives the stack: `RemovalPolicy.RETAIN` and deletion
  protection. [test: says what happens to stateful resources; review]

## The AWS SDK v3

- Import only the clients you use (`@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb`), never a
  whole SDK; esbuild bundles them into the {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}server{{ else }}function{{ end }}. [review]
- Pin every `@aws-sdk/*` package to the same exact version. [review]
- A client's region and credentials come from the {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}instance (its role, through IMDSv2, and `AWS_REGION`){{ else }}Lambda environment{{ end }}; never
  pass keys or set a region in code. [review]
- Wrap each service in one module behind an interface the rest of the code
  depends on; tests fake that interface. [review]
- The SDK retries throttling and transient errors itself; don't add a retry
  loop around it. Catch what you can answer (a failed condition is a 409),
  let the rest reach {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}`answer`'s{{ else }}the handler's{{ end }} 500. [review]

{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}## The network and the load balancer

- The VPC has isolated subnets in two zones and no NAT gateway or internet
  gateway: the instances reach S3 (their code, the operating system's
  packages), DynamoDB and CloudWatch Logs through VPC endpoints, and nothing
  else. A new AWS service the code calls needs its endpoint. [test; review]
- The VPC's CIDR is written out (`VPC_CIDR`), so security group rules name
  it and CDK Nag can read them. [nag: AwsSolutions-EC23]
- Flow logs record rejected traffic to a log group with a retention.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-VPC7]
- The load balancer is internal, its security group open only to the VPC on
  its listener's port, and the instances' only to the load balancer on the
  server's port. Making it internet-facing is an issue's decision: it then
  needs HTTPS with a certificate and an authorizer in front of the routes.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-EC23; review]
- It drops invalid header fields and writes access logs to an S3 bucket
  (SSE-S3, TLS only, public access blocked, kept 90 days) that the log
  delivery service may write to. [nag: AwsSolutions-ELB2, AwsSolutions-S10;
  test]
- The target group checks `GET /health` and gives a stopping instance 30
  seconds to finish its requests. [test]

## EC2 instances and the Auto Scaling group

- A launch template, never a launch configuration: Amazon Linux 2023 on
  `arm64` (Graviton: cheaper and lower energy), resolved at deploy time from
  the public SSM parameter, so synth needs no lookup. [review]
- IMDSv2 required, the root volume gp3 and encrypted, detailed monitoring
  on. [nag: AwsSolutions-EC26, AwsSolutions-EC28; test]
- The instances' own role, granted only what the server calls and the
  `s3:GetObject` of its own code; no AWS managed policy, no SSH key, no
  inbound rule but the load balancer's. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM4,
  AwsSolutions-IAM5; test]
- The server ships as an S3 asset: `infra/server_asset.ts` bundles
  `src/main.ts` with the installed esbuild into one `server.mjs` during
  synth; never Docker. The user data downloads it, installs Node.js and the
  CloudWatch agent from the Amazon Linux repositories, and runs it under
  systemd as its own user. A change to the boot is a change to
  `bootCommands` and its test. [review]
- At least two instances in two zones, a maximum set, a cooldown, a
  target-tracking scaling policy, and the load balancer's health check
  replacing an instance that fails it. [nag: AwsSolutions-AS1,
  AwsSolutions-AS2; test]
- A rolling update replaces one instance at a time and keeps one in
  service. It sends no resource signals (that would need a CloudFormation
  endpoint), so it waits a fixed time per instance and doesn't see whether
  the new one is healthy: a release that can't start replaces every
  instance. Test the boot before a release. [test; review]
- systemd restarts the server whenever it stops, and logrotate keeps its
  log file small on the disk; a change to the boot keeps both. [test]
- No secrets in the user data or the unit's environment: they are visible
  to anyone who can describe the launch template. Read them from Secrets
  Manager at start-up. [review]

{{ else }}## API Gateway HTTP API

- Every route is authorized: IAM (`HttpIamAuthorizer`), a JWT authorizer or a
  Lambda authorizer. [nag: AwsSolutions-APIG4]
- The stage writes access logs to a log group with a retention.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-APIG1; test]
- The stage is throttled (`throttle: { rateLimit, burstLimit }`), so a burst
  can't run up the bill or starve other functions. [review]
- Routes name their method and path (`GET /items/{id}`); no `ANY /{proxy+}`
  that hands routing to the function. [review]

## Lambda configuration in the stack

- `NodejsFunction`, bundled by the installed esbuild
  (`forceDockerBundling: false`); never Docker, which CI and agents lack.
  [review]
- The newest Node.js runtime. [nag: AwsSolutions-L1]
- `arm64` (Graviton): cheaper per millisecond and lower energy. [review]
- Memory and timeout set for the work, the timeout under API Gateway's 30
  seconds. [review]
- Its own role, granted only what the code calls; no AWS managed policies.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM4]
- No wildcard permissions: grants (`table.grant(fn, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`),
  never a hand-written `*`. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM5; test: no wildcard action]
- No secrets in environment variables: they are visible in the console and
  the template. Read them from Secrets Manager at start-up. [review]

{{ end }}## The CDK app

- `infra/main.ts` and `infra/standard.ts` belong to the Standard; the
  Framework's stacks are in `infra/stacks.ts`, which `defineStacks` returns.
  [review]
- Stacks are environment-agnostic: no `env` from `CDK_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT`, no
  `fromLookup`, so synth needs no account. [review]
- Every stack carries the Standard's tags (`c4factory:project`,
  `c4factory:system`, `c4factory:container`). [test: tags every stack]
- Every stateful resource says its removal policy. [test]
- A CDK Nag finding is fixed in the resource. An acknowledgement
  (`Validations.of(construct).acknowledge({ id, reason })`), on the construct
  the finding's path names or an ancestor of it, names one finding
  on one construct, with a reason specific to this system. [review]
- `cdk.json`'s feature flags are the ones `cdk init` recommends for the
  pinned CDK; a task never turns one off. [review]

## Tests

- No test calls AWS, opens a socket {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}other than on loopback {{ else }}{{ end }}or needs credentials. [review]
- Stacks are tested with `Template.fromStack` from `aws-cdk-lib/assertions`:
  `hasResourceProperties`, `resourceCountIs`, `Match`. Assert what the
  issue needs (a route, a grant, a setting), not the whole template. [review]
- Code that calls AWS is tested through its interface with a fake that
  records commands and answers from memory. [review]
{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}- Request handling is tested by calling `answer` with a request and checking
  the status code and body; `src/server.ts` over HTTP on a loopback port the
  system picks. [review]{{ else }}- Handlers are tested by building an `APIGatewayProxyEventV2` and checking
  the status code and body. [review]{{ end }}
